---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// PITCHGRAPH — the intonation trace, as its own page.
//
// THE NAME IS SETTLED, and the route to it is worth recording because I got the PROCESS
// wrong first. Asked for name ideation, I ran it, then treated the ideating agent's own
// favourite ("readings") as the decision and hard-renamed everything. The owner had asked
// for ideation AND a /proofs chooser — the picking was his. So the chooser was built
// afterwards (work/understand/readings-name/), rendering all eight candidates in the four
// places a name actually appears: the engraved plate, the browser tab, the index row among
// its real siblings, and the URL.
//
// Shown the sheet, he kept "pitchgraph": "its fine that its similar. its a similar product
// after all."
//
// That answers the strongest argument against it, which was that "pitch graph" is the
// generic category label four shipping products already use for this figure
// (pitchreader.com, pitchdiagram.com, Ocavis, MusicalBoard). Reading like a category is
// only a fault if you are claiming to be something else. This IS one of those — a figure
// of pitch over time — and a name that says so costs a reader nothing. The counter-case
// for "readings" is on the sheet if it is ever reopened: it names the artifact the way
// "proofs" does, and the row of per-note panels IS a set of readings.
//
// BOTH URLS 301 to this one, so a third name would cost one more redirect and nothing else.
//
// WHAT IT IS. You play; it draws how far off the note you are, in cents, over time. Two
// figures at once, which is the decision this page exists to execute:
//
//   LIVE     one continuous trace, cents off the nearest note, ±50¢, over a 0¢ datum.
//            Inside ±3¢ the ink goes accent — the same in-tune window the tuner ships.
//   REVIEW   as each note ENDS, a small panel appears in a row beneath, one per note,
//            each with its own 0¢ datum and its mean offset engraved on it. The row
//            accumulates: the page becomes a record of the phrase you just played.
//
// The owner picked "both at once, panels build up as you go" over "panels appear on
// release". The cost is real and worth stating rather than hiding: two figures compete
// for attention while you play, and the research says the display is already where
// attention is lost (Pardue & McPherson 2019 gaze-tracked beginning violinists looking at
// visuals 16% of the time; the one head-to-head of trace vs per-note verdicts found no
// difference, p=0.41, while every group played WORSE with a live screen). The mitigation
// here is hierarchy, not omission: the live trace is the figure, the panel row is set
// small and quiet beneath it, and a finished panel never moves again.
//
// WHY THE PANELS ARE THE CONVENTIONAL HALF. Per-note panels are how every shipped tool
// that reports real intonation statistics does it — Tunable's per-note cards and
// TonalEnergy's tile grid are the only two places in a survey of nine tuners, five
// practice platforms and six editors where a per-note mean and spread actually live.
// Cents-over-time is the conventional LIVE view. Tunable, TonalEnergy and Singing Carrots
// all ship both. So this page is not inventing a shape; it is putting the two halves on
// one screen.
//
// THE AXIS PROBLEM, AND WHY THE SEGMENTER IS A MODULE. "Cents off the nearest note" is
// discontinuous: move A4 → B4 and the reading wraps +49 → −49 without passing zero, so a
// clean slur draws the same full-height vertical line a catastrophic error would. Deciding
// WHEN the note changed is therefore load-bearing, and it has no answer that is right
// everywhere. It lives in src/lib/practice-room/segment.ts as a registry of eight named,
// parameterised mechanisms with `node tools/segment-bench.mjs` to score them. This page
// picks one; changing it is one string.
//
// Picks executed here, from work/understand/practice-room-pitch-change/ (sheet 2) and
// work/understand/practice-room-centline/ (sheet 1, Q3/Q4 still open):
//   Q1/07  a panel per note, width in proportion to how long you held it
//   Q2/05  hysteresis + dwell (the bench prefers hysteresis alone; see segment.ts)
//   Q3/05  the mean printed ON the drawing, not in a readout beneath it
//
// Engine reuse: the same MPM detector and note math the practice room runs, at the same
// 33 ms cadence. Nothing is recorded and nothing leaves the page.
//
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE PRACTICE ROOM'S IDIOM, ADOPTED WHOLE (2026-08-08, the owner: "align
// practice.andrewshiau.com/pitchgraph/ ui with practice.andrewshiau? like put stuff in
// boxes, use the new control for start/stop, etc.")
//
// This page copied the practice room's shape once, in early August, and then the room moved
// on three times while this one did not — so the two tools on the same host had drifted into
// two different design systems. What the room has that this page did not, measured on both
// built pages with `tools/probe-room-vs-pitchgraph.mjs` before anything was changed:
//
//   1. IT IS A SCREEN, NOT A DOCUMENT. `#mt-app` is `round(down, 100dvh, --lead)` and
//      escapes `.wrap`'s 1120px measure. This page sat inside the measure, so its case was
//      1064px wide on a 1440px screen and the document scrolled 10px (docH 910 of 900) — an
//      instrument whose transport can leave the viewport.
//   2. A NAMED ROW LADDER, stated in leads, with the case a `subgrid` on it. This page laid
//      out in flow with margins, so nothing here was on the 28px lead by construction.
//   3. A BOX PER CONTROL GROUP, named in its own top edge — the sub-cases treatment
//      (chooser practice-room-panels pick 03). Here `a4 440 hz` floated unnamed and, worse,
//      flush RIGHT, while the room puts the identical control flush LEFT. Same control,
//      opposite corners, on two pages of one instrument.
//   4. THE RAMS SWITCH for the transport (transport chooser treatment 05, "the snow-white
//      dot"), 1.45× larger since 2026-08-07: a hairline field and a dot that travels and
//      takes the accent, with NO WORD. This page still had the two-pocket track plus the
//      word `start`, which is the control the room deleted — and the elaborate fix this
//      file used to carry (absolute-positioning the track outside the button so the WORD
//      landed on the axis) existed only to rescue a word the newer control does not have.
//   5. THE VERB IN THE READING'S ROW, centred by a real `1fr auto 1fr` middle column. Here
//      it was its own row two rows down, under the panels.
//   6. THE CALIBRATION SCRUBS. In the room `a4` and `hz` are drag handles (chooser
//      metrotuner-scrub-target pick 03: the unit is the scrubber, the field only types).
//      Here they were inert labels — the same control, looking identical, behaving
//      differently, which is worse than either choice on its own.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT COPIED, and why each is a simplification rather than an omission:
//   · NO SNAP SCROLLER, NO PAGE WORDS, NO DRAWN PHONE FRAME. Those exist because the room
//     holds THREE cases that become three swipe pages. This page has one case, so its
//     border can simply stay at every width, and the two-leads-of-`case-top` the phone
//     needed (to clear the metronome's accent mark) is not needed either.
//   · NO EMPTY `foot` ROW. The room's cases declare rows they do not fill because three
//     cases share one ladder and the empty modules are what hold them in phase. There is
//     nothing here to be in phase with, and the room's own rule applies: a row of the
//     ladder that nothing occupies is 28px of dead air pretending to be structure (that is
//     why `[transport]` was deleted there). This page's ladder has a `panels` row the room
//     does not have, and no `foot` row, because that is what its content is.
//   · THE TOP DATUM IS FOLDED INTO THE CASE'S OWN OVERLAY. In the room it is drawn on
//     `.mt-pages::before` because one line has to cross three cases, which forces the
//     gradient's inset to restate the scroller's padding in a second place. One case needs
//     no such line, so it becomes a third gradient on `.pg-case::after` and is measured
//     from the case itself — one fewer number that can drift.
//
// THE COST, stated plainly because it is the real one: the box, the notch, the switch and
// the scrub handle are DUPLICATED here rather than shared. They cannot be shared today —
// the room's copies live in its own Astro-scoped `<style>`, so their selectors carry a
// `data-astro-cid` this page's elements will never have, and lifting them into global.css
// would mean renaming five families of class in a 4000-line file that eight harnesses
// select on. So the duplication is paid for with a CHECK instead of a promise:
// `tools/verify-pitchgraph-parity.mjs` measures both built pages and fails by name when a
// token, a box measure or the switch's geometry stops agreeing. Config that can drift will
// drift; assert it.
---

<Layout
  title="pitchgraph — Andrew Shiau"
  description="Play, and watch how far off the note you are in cents. Pitch detection runs on-device; the microphone signal never leaves the page."
  onPracticeHost
>
  <!-- THE INSTRUMENT IS THE PAGE. No dek, no explanation, no colophon — the same shape
       the practice room has, whose h1 is visually hidden and whose only prose is a mic
       error when there is one. This page had both a dek and a closing note; the owner:
       "i dont want to see this". Right: a tool that explains itself every time you open it
       is a tool you are reading instead of playing. The instrument's plate names it, the
       axis labels say which way is sharp, and the first note you play shows what the
       panel row is for. All the prose moved to /work/pitchgraph/.
       The h1 stays for screen readers and the document outline. -->
  <div id="pg-app">
    <h1 class="pg-sr-h1">pitchgraph</h1>

    <!-- ── THE SCREEN ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The room's `.mt-pages` with one column instead of three: the grid that owns the
         row ladder. The case is a subgrid on it, so every row this page states is a row
         of the screen rather than a margin between two blocks. -->
    <div class="pg-screen" id="pg-screen">
      <!-- ONE HAIRLINE CASE, the site's own idiom (the embed-frame argument: inside is a
           machine, so the border is sanctioned where the site otherwise draws rules not
           boxes). The name interrupts the top frame like an engraved plate. -->
      <div class="pg-case">
        <span class="pg-plate" aria-hidden="true">pitchgraph</span>

        <!-- THE SPEC ROW: the one set-once control, in its own named box, flush LEFT —
             where the room puts the identical control on both of its cases that carry it.
             `.f` and the global `.f input` rule are what the room's own calibration field
             uses, so the field itself is genuinely shared rather than copied: one
             definition in global.css sets its 16px (iOS focus-zoom floor), its 44px
             target and its underline. -->
        <div class="pg-ctop">
          <span class="f pg-a4 pg-grp" id="pg-a4-scrub">
            <span class="pg-gl" aria-hidden="true">calibration</span>
            <label for="pg-a4" class="pg-lbl pg-hd"
              title="drag ↔ to change the calibration">a4</label>
            <input
              type="number" id="pg-a4" data-testid="a4" inputmode="decimal"
              value="440" min="400" max="480" step="1" title="type a value"
              aria-label="A4 calibration in hertz — type a value, or drag the a4 or hz label horizontally"
            /><span class="pg-lbl pg-hd" data-testid="a4-handle"
              title="drag ↔ to change the calibration">hz</span>
          </span>
        </div>

        <!-- THE FIGURE: the live trace. Drawn to a fixed viewBox measured from the
             element so 1px means 1px — a viewBox narrower than the element with
             preserveAspectRatio="none" stretches every label horizontally. -->
        <div class="pg-fig" id="pg-fig">
          <svg id="pg-svg" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"></svg>
        </div>

        <!-- ── THE READING AND THE VERB ARE ONE ROW, THE VERB ON THE AXIS ──────────
             The room's `.mt-read`, verbatim in structure: three explicit columns,
             `1fr auto 1fr`, so the middle column IS the case's centre axis by
             construction and whatever sits in it is centred however wide the note or the
             cents get. The transport was its own row here, below the panels; a verb two
             rows from the reading it belongs to is what the room's transport chooser
             (Q1/02) fixed on that page a week ago.
             The reading keeps its flush edges: the note reads from the left inset, the
             cents/Hz group to the right one. -->
        <div class="pg-read">
          <span class="pg-note" id="pg-note" data-testid="note">—</span>
          <!-- THE RAMS SWITCH (transport chooser treatment 05, at the 1.45× size the
               control-size chooser picked): a hairline field and one dot that travels to
               the far side and takes the accent. NO WORD — the control is one mark, so
               the mark is the only thing to centre and the grid column above does it.
               State is form AND hue: the dot moves 16px and goes accent, because colour
               alone measures 3.54:1 between --ink and --faint, which the accent-clarity
               chooser already rejected at small sizes.
               `aria-label` carries the whole verb now that there is no word to read. -->
          <div class="pg-cap">
            <button
              type="button" class="pg-btn pg-latch"
              id="pg-listen" data-testid="listen-toggle"
              aria-pressed="false" aria-label="start listening"
            >
              <svg class="pg-rd" width="32" height="16" viewBox="0 0 32 16" aria-hidden="true">
                <rect class="pg-fld" x="0.75" y="0.75" width="30.5" height="14.5"></rect>
                <circle class="pg-dt" cx="8" cy="8" r="3.2"></circle>
              </svg>
            </button>
          </div>
          <!-- The cents and the Hz stay ONE group on the right — one reading at two
               precisions, so the Hz appearing must not move the cents. -->
          <span class="pg-rr">
            <span class="pg-cents" id="pg-cents" data-testid="cents">–</span>
            <span class="pg-hz" id="pg-hz" data-testid="hz"></span>
          </span>
        </div>

        <!-- THE REVIEW ROW: one panel per finished note, filling in as you play. Its own
             row of the ladder, directly under the reading, so the three figures of this
             instrument — trace, reading+verb, record — sit against each other and the
             slack is placed above and below the group. That is the room's composition fix
             (its first ladder gave the figure row all the leftover and the readout landed
             215px under the dial it describes). -->
        <div class="pg-panels" id="pg-panels" data-testid="panels" aria-live="off"></div>

        <!-- errors only, on its own reserved row. An idle hint here was deleted from the
             practice room as ugly; this channel carries mic denials and nothing else. The
             row exists whether or not it has words, so starting and stopping cannot
             reflow the case. -->
        <div class="pg-hintline"><span class="pg-hint" id="pg-hint" data-testid="hint" role="status"></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ONE WORD OUT, at the foot, the way the practice room carries its page words: its
         own band below the screen, `flex:none`, so it is outside the case rather than a row
         of the instrument. Not a sentence about the microphone and not a description of the
         other tool — a way back to the rest of the tools, and nothing else.
         TWO LEADS EXACTLY (one of line, one of air beneath), which is what keeps the screen
         a whole number of leads after the foot takes its share. -->
    <div class="pg-foot">
      <a href="https://practice.andrewshiau.com/">practice room</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  /* THE ONE ESCAPE, the room's rule with this page's id. A screen cannot sit inside the
     document's 1120px measure — and `.wrap`'s own `calc(var(--unit)*5)` bottom padding is
     what made this page scroll 10px past a 900px viewport while claiming to be an
     instrument. `:has(#pg-app)` scopes the override to exactly this page: the rule ships in
     this page's CSS chunk and the selector matches nothing anywhere else. */
  .wrap:has(#pg-app){max-width:none;padding:0}
  main:has(#pg-app){display:block}

  /* ⌥G ON THIS PAGE DRAWS THE INSTRUMENT'S DATUMS, not the document guide — and this is
     REQUIRED rather than tidy, because the guide's twelve bands are the 1120px measure's
     columns and the rule above deletes that measure. Left alone, the persisted grid
     (⌥G survives across pages in localStorage) would draw a column system this page no
     longer has. Global for the reason the room records: Astro would pin the html/body half
     of the selector to a scope class those elements never carry. */
  body:has(#pg-app) .guide{display:none !important}
  :is(html,body).showgrid .pg-case::after{opacity:1}

  /* THE SVG STYLES CANNOT BE SCOPED, and this was a real bug, not a precaution.
     Astro scopes a component's CSS by stamping a `data-astro-cid-*` attribute onto the
     elements it compiles, then compiling the selectors to match it. Every mark in both
     figures is built at RUNTIME with innerHTML, so those elements never carry the
     attribute and every scoped rule missed them: the traces computed to
     `stroke: none; stroke-width: 1px`, the fills fell back to black, and the figure
     rendered as a solid slab with the numbers correct underneath it. A screenshot found
     it in one look; no DOM assertion could, because the geometry was right.
     So the drawing's rules live in `is:global`, namespaced under `.pg-case` so they
     cannot leak to another page's SVG. */
  .pg-case .pg-datum{stroke:var(--ink);stroke-width:1;fill:none}
  .pg-case .pg-edge{stroke:var(--faint);stroke-width:1;stroke-dasharray:2 3;fill:none}
  /* The two practice bands, after Singing Carrots: ±5¢ is "barely noticeable even by
     most trained ears", ±12¢ "commonly audible by most untrained ears". Fields rather
     than rules because in-tune is a PLACE you are inside, not a number to compare. */
  /* MEASURED DOWN FROM .10/.05 AFTER LOOKING: at those values the two bands read as one
     grey slab across the middle of the figure, the ±12¢ edge was invisible against the
     ±5¢, and the trace — the actual subject — was the faintest thing on the screen. The
     bands are context; they must lose to ink. Drawn as a HAIRLINE EDGE plus a very light
     wash instead of a heavier wash: the boundary is the information ("am I inside?"), and
     an edge states it at 1px where a fill needs 20% to say the same thing badly. */
  .pg-case .pg-band-in{fill:var(--accent);opacity:.07;stroke:none}
  .pg-case .pg-band-out{fill:var(--accent);opacity:.03;stroke:none}
  .pg-case .pg-band-edge{stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1;fill:none;opacity:.35}
  .pg-case .pg-trace{fill:none;stroke:var(--ink);stroke-width:1.5;stroke-linejoin:round;
    stroke-linecap:round}
  .pg-case .pg-trace.pg-in{stroke:var(--accent)}
  .pg-case .pg-axl{font:inherit;font-size:11px;fill:var(--faint);stroke:none}
  .pg-case .pg-pname{font:inherit;font-size:11px;fill:var(--ink);stroke:none}
  .pg-case .pg-pmean{font:inherit;font-size:11px;fill:var(--dim);stroke:none}
  .pg-case .pg-pdatum{stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:1;fill:none}
  .pg-case .pg-ptrace{fill:none;stroke:var(--ink);stroke-width:1.2;stroke-linejoin:round}
  .pg-case .pg-ptrace.pg-in{stroke:var(--accent)}
  /* A one-read note has no line to draw — a dot is the honest mark for it. */
  .pg-case .pg-pdot{fill:var(--ink);stroke:none}

  /* SAME SCOPING TRAP, SECOND VICTIM. `.visually-hidden` is defined inside
     practice-room.astro's own scoped block, so it does not exist here at all — and a
     replacement defined in THIS page's scoped block would not reach the span either,
     because the panel is built with innerHTML. Both mistakes shipped in one pass and both
     rendered the same way: "A4, +20.0 cents, 1221 milliseconds" as visible copy under the
     row. Global, namespaced under .pg-panel. */
  .pg-panel .pg-sr{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;
    clip-path:inset(50%);white-space:nowrap}

  /* ══ THE PANEL ITSELF, AND WHY ITS RULES ARE HERE ═══════════════════════════════════════
     SAME SCOPING TRAP, THIRD VICTIM — and this one had SHIPPED, live, since the day the panel
     row was built. The rules below sat in the scoped block, where Astro compiled them to
     `.pg-panel[data-astro-cid-…]`; a panel is built with `createElement` in the script, so it
     carries no such attribute and NOT ONE of them ever matched. Measured on the live page and
     on the local build, both identical: `borderLeft: 0px, padding: 0px, minWidth: auto,
     display: block`. The record row has been drawing its panels with no dividers, no gutters
     and no minimum width the whole time.
     WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE: none of those rules changes a number any test asserted, and the row
     still LOOKS like a row of small figures — the panels are `flex:none` on a flex parent with
     a `gap:1px`, which is enough to keep them apart and in order. The two things that were
     actually missing are the hairline that separates one reading from the next, and the 14px
     floor under a one-read note. So the failure mode was a quieter drawing, not a broken one,
     which is exactly the kind that survives.
     THE RULE THIS PAGE ALREADY STATES TWICE: markup this page creates in JS needs its CSS in
     the `is:global` chunk. It was written for the SVG marks and for `.pg-sr`, and the panel's
     own box was left behind both times.
     ── AND THE DRAWINGS SIT AT THE ROW'S BOTTOM (found by shooting the page mid-phrase).
     THE REASON THIS WAS WRITTEN FOR IS GONE, and it is recorded rather than quietly kept: the
     switch's ink used to carry `translateY(9.5px)`, which hung it 3.5px BELOW its own 28px row
     and put the overhang in the row that holds these panels, so the panels were pushed to the
     END to move the row's spare lead up to meet it. That nudge is deleted (see `.pg-rd` below —
     the row was already centring the mark, and the 9.5 was the defect the annotation "this
     needs to be in line with text" was about). There is no overhang to absorb now.
     `align-items:flex-end` STAYS, on its own merit and not as a compensation: the row is 3
     leads and its drawings are 2, so a lead of it is slack either way, and a panel that grows
     should grow upward from the reading it belongs to. It also cannot have moved with the
     nudge's removal — a `transform` takes no part in layout, so the only thing that changed is
     where the switch's ink paints, and the panels' boxes and the row's height are the same
     numbers before and after (the spec's `panels` row and panel-height checks pass either way).
     Rejected: a gap between the rows (a number off the ladder for a problem the ladder's own
     slack already covers). */
  .pg-panel{flex:none;position:relative;border-left:1px solid var(--line);
    padding:0 2px;min-width:14px;
    display:flex;align-items:flex-end}
  .pg-panel:first-child{border-left:0}
  .pg-panel svg{display:block;height:calc(var(--lead) * 2);width:100%;overflow:visible}
  /* The phone's shorter record row, restated here because the rule it overrides now lives in
     this block — left in the scoped one it would have been inert for the same reason. */
  @media (max-width: 1001px){
    .pg-panel svg{height:calc(var(--lead) * 1.5)}
  }
</style>

<style>
  /* Tokens are the site's, unchanged. Every measure here is --lead or a multiple. */
  /* The h1 is for the outline and screen readers; the plate on the case is the visible
     name. Same call the practice room makes. */
  .pg-sr-h1{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;
    clip-path:inset(50%);white-space:nowrap;margin:0}

  /* ── THE SCREEN'S HEIGHT IS SNAPPED TO THE LEADING, the room's rule verbatim.
     `100dvh` is whatever the window happens to be, and a screen whose height is not a
     multiple of --lead cannot put its rows on a 28px ladder — the remainder has to go
     somewhere, and wherever it goes is off-grid. The bare `100dvh` first is the fallback
     for anything without `round()` (Chrome 125, Safari 15.4, Firefox 118): those get an
     unsnapped screen rather than no height at all.
     THE TOKENS ARE NAMED AFTER THE ROOM'S, one for one, because the parity harness pairs
     them by name — `--pg-inset` against `--mt-inset`, and so on. A token renamed here
     without being renamed there is a drift the harness reports rather than absorbs.
     `--pg-inset` is 3ch and not `--gutter`: the clearance between a case's frame and the
     ink inside it is a CHARACTER COUNT (MIL-STD-1472G §5.2.3.14.12 states legend clearance
     in the width of the font's own capital), and in IBM Plex Mono at `--step` 1ch is
     exactly 9.000px — so at 3ch the frame is a cell boundary and the frame and the type
     are one ladder instead of two that never meet. 28px is 3.111 characters. */
  #pg-app{--pg-inset:3ch;
    /* the `case-top` row's height, read by the ⌥G top datum so the line and the row it
       names are one number */
    --pg-case-top:var(--lead);
    /* how far the group box hangs outside the ink it contains: the box pads out and is
       pulled back by this, so the ink lands on `--pg-inset` and the box's RULE lands one
       character inside the frame, which is the datum ⌥G draws. See `.pg-grp`. */
    --pg-box-pull:18px;
    height:100dvh;height:round(down, 100dvh, var(--lead));
    display:flex;flex-direction:column;overflow:hidden}

  /* ── THE ROW LADDER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One grid, rows named and stated in leads, and the case is `subgrid` on it — so a row
     is a row of the SCREEN and the content cannot disagree about where it is. This
     replaces flow-plus-margins, where every interval was whatever the previous block's
     height happened to leave.
     THE HEIGHTS ARE DERIVED, not chosen:
       · `spec` is 2 leads because the calibration box's chrome is 18 + 8 + 2 borders = 28
         and its content is one 44px field — the same 2 leads the room's spec row takes,
         with the box overhanging into the slack below exactly as it does there.
       · `read` and `hint` are one lead each: 19px of ink apiece.
       · `panels` is 3 leads, which is the row this page has and the room does not.
       · `figure` is `auto` so the trace sets its own height (`.pg-fig`, 7 leads) — pinning
         it to a row count would either crop the drawing or open a gap inside the group.
     THE SLACK IS TWO ROWS, and that is the room's hard-won composition rule: giving the
     figure row all the leftover centres the drawing in a tall row and leaves its own
     readout stranded hundreds of pixels below it. Placed air above and below the group
     instead, in equal `1fr` rows, so trace, reading and record sit against each other.
     NO `foot` ROW. The room declares rows its cases do not fill because three cases share
     one ladder and the empty modules hold them in phase; this page has one case, and its
     own rule against dead rows applies (that is why `[transport]` was deleted there).
     The vertical padding is 3 leads (2 above, 1 below), so the ladder still resolves to a
     whole number of leads after the padding takes its share. */
  .pg-screen{flex:1;min-height:0;position:relative;
    display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0, 1fr);
    padding:var(--group) var(--group) var(--lead);
    grid-template-rows:
      [case-top]  var(--lead)            /* the case's own top air */
      [spec]      calc(var(--lead) * 2)  /* the calibration box */
      [slack-a]   minmax(0, 1fr)         /* placed air, above the instrument */
      [figure]    auto                   /* the trace, at its own height */
      [read]      var(--lead)            /* the reading AND the verb */
      [panels]    calc(var(--lead) * 3)  /* the record */
      [slack-b]   minmax(0, 1fr)         /* placed air, below it */
      [hint]      var(--lead)            /* mic errors, and nothing else */
      [case-bot]  var(--lead)            /* the case's own bottom air */
      [case-end]}

  /* THE CASE. The room's `.mt-half`: a 1px --line hairline (the weakest mark the system
     has — the case is not interactive, so it must be the quietest line on the screen), the
     plate interrupting the top frame, and the ladder inherited.
     THE VERTICAL PADDING IS GONE, and that is the substantive part rather than a tidy-up:
     padding on a subgrid item does not move the inherited row lines, so keeping the old
     `var(--lead) 3ch` would have pushed every row off the ladder it inherits. The air is
     the `case-top` and `case-bot` ROWS now — the same 28px, held where the composition can
     see it. Horizontal padding stays: it insets content from the hairline on the axis the
     ladder says nothing about.
     ONE EXPLICIT COLUMN, which is a fix and not a formality: with rows named and columns
     left to `auto`, grid auto-placement is free to invent an implicit second column and
     place a row in it — measured on the room, where the metronome's foot line landed 414px
     to the right of everything else. */
  .pg-case{position:relative;
    border:1px solid var(--line);
    grid-row:case-top / case-end;
    display:grid;grid-template-rows:subgrid;
    grid-template-columns:minmax(0, 1fr);
    padding:0 var(--pg-inset)}
  .pg-plate{position:absolute;top:-14px;left:var(--unit);
    background:var(--paper);padding:0 1ch;color:var(--faint)}

  /* ── THE SPEC ROW ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `align-self:start` and not `center`: the box is 73px of 44px targets inside a 56px
     row, and centring a taller box in its row puts its TYPE half the difference away from
     where the row line is — which is the residual misalignment the room measured between
     its two cases' spec rows. Aligned to the row's start, the box keeps its full height
     and overhangs downward into the figure's slack, so no tap target shrinks. */
  .pg-ctop{grid-row:spec;align-self:start;
    display:flex;align-items:baseline;justify-content:flex-start;
    gap:var(--lead) 4ch;flex-wrap:wrap}

  /* ── A BOX PER CONTROL GROUP, NAMED IN ITS OWN TOP EDGE ──────────────────────
     The room's sub-cases treatment (chooser practice-room-panels pick 03, held against a
     seven-option riff). Its premise is the owner's: "practice is diff tool than website so
     the rule about no lines and stuff doesnt rly work" — STYLE.md's "structure is rules,
     not boxes" was written for a DOCUMENT you read, and this is an INSTRUMENT you operate
     while holding a violin, glanced at from a stand, where boxes that group controls are
     the native idiom of hardware.
     IT INVENTS NOTHING AND IT DELETES A MARK: the notch is the case's own engraved plate at
     a smaller scale (same -14px offset, same --faint, same paper knock-out), and
     `calibration` stops being a loose word beside a row to become the row's name.
     ONE BOX ON THIS PAGE, and that is the stated bound rather than an oversight. The
     room's own recorded cost is that a notched name means "this is an instrument" once per
     case, and five more per screen means "this is a group" while the case's plate stops
     being special. This page has exactly one control group — the calibration — so it gets
     exactly one box. The trace, the reading and the panel row are FIGURES and a reading,
     not control groups; a box round a figure would be the second frame the phone rule
     exists to avoid.
     THE ARITHMETIC IS THE ROOM'S, and both halves of it matter:
       · 18 + 8 + 2 borders = 28 = one whole lead, so a boxed row lands on the baseline
         grid. The sheet's own 20/10 came to 32, which is not a multiple of 28.
       · rule = case inset − pull, ink = rule + border + padding. For BOTH to be whole 9px
         cells, `border + padding` must be a multiple of 9 — so padding 17, not 18, with the
         1px border making up the character. At 18 the rule lands on 1ch and the ink drifts
         to 3.111ch, which is the finding half-applied.
     The pull-back is this page's own idiom too (`.pg-hd` and the room's meter digits do the
     same): pad out to the box, then pull the padding back with a matching negative margin,
     so the ink sits where the datum wants it and the box overhangs into the case's air,
     which is nothing. */
  .pg-grp{position:relative;border:1px solid var(--line);
    --pg-box-pad:17px;
    padding:18px var(--pg-box-pad) 8px;
    margin-left:calc(var(--pg-box-pull) * -1);margin-right:calc(var(--pg-box-pull) * -1)}
  /* The notch travels with the box's padding, so its glyph lands on 4ch: 9 (rule) + 1
     (border) + 17 (pad) = 27, plus its own 1ch of knock-out. */
  .pg-gl{position:absolute;top:-14px;left:var(--pg-box-pad);background:var(--paper);
    padding:0 1ch;color:var(--faint);line-height:var(--lead)}

  /* ── THE CALIBRATION FIELD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     BASELINE, NOT CENTRE: the group holds a 15px unit label beside a 16px input (`.f input`
     is 16px, forced by iOS Safari's focus zoom), and `align-items:center` would make the
     GROUP's first baseline the taller input's, so its type would sit below every other
     baseline in the row. On `baseline` the label and the numeral share one, and the group
     reports it as its own.
     The input's own rules come from global.css's `.f input` — the same single definition
     the room's calibration field uses, so the field is genuinely shared rather than copied.
     What is stated here is only what the room states: the width and the centring. */
  .pg-a4{display:inline-flex;align-items:baseline;gap:1ch}
  .pg-a4 input{flex:0 0 auto;width:6ch;text-align:center;
    -moz-appearance:textfield;appearance:textfield}
  .pg-a4 input::-webkit-outer-spin-button,
  .pg-a4 input::-webkit-inner-spin-button{-webkit-appearance:none;margin:0}
  /* The field types; the UNIT scrubs (chooser metrotuner-scrub-target pick 03 — Figma's
     model, and the owner's words on that sheet: "not on the typing input bc its confusing
     that u can type"). `cursor:text` is stated rather than left to the UA so the pixels
     cannot inherit a lie about what they do. */
  .pg-a4 input{cursor:text}
  /* 44px BY PADDING, and the number is the room's measured one: `a4` and `hz` are 2ch =
     18px bare, so 14px each side takes them to 46 × 44. The negative margins keep the
     visual line identical — the target grows, the composition does not move. */
  .pg-hd{cursor:ew-resize;touch-action:none;
    display:inline-block;padding:8px 14px;margin:-8px -14px}
  .pg-hd:focus-visible{outline:1px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:2px}
  .pg-lbl{color:var(--faint)}
  /* While a scrub is live the digits and the handle in hand read accent — that flash, the
     resize cursor and the title are the whole affordance now that the value rails are gone. */
  .pg-a4.scrubbing input{color:var(--accent)}
  .pg-a4.scrubbing .pg-hd.hot{color:var(--accent)}

  /* THE FIGURE. Fixed height in leads so the trace has a stable scale and the panel
     row below it never shifts — a figure that resizes with its content makes every
     finished panel move, which is the one thing a record must not do.
     `align-self:center` inside its own row, which is the one place centring is still
     right: a drawing in a taller row is a figure in a module. */
  .pg-fig{grid-row:figure;align-self:center;
    width:100%;height:calc(var(--lead) * 7)}
  .pg-fig svg{display:block;width:100%;height:100%;overflow:visible}

  /* ── THREE COLUMNS, BECAUSE THE MIDDLE HAS TO BE THE AXIS ────────────────────
     `1fr auto 1fr` makes the centre column the axis by construction: the two side columns
     are equal because they are declared equal, so whatever sits in the middle is centred
     no matter how wide the note or the cents get. The old flush spread — one `.pg-ends`
     spacer with `flex:1` — had no middle at all, so a verb dropped into it would have got
     a slot in the spread rather than the axis the figure above radiates from.
     `align-self:start` pairs with `.pg-cap`'s: the room measured that changing only one of
     the two puts the interval at 24.50 or 32.50 instead of one lead. */
  .pg-read{grid-row:read;align-self:start;
    display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto 1fr;align-items:baseline;column-gap:2ch}
  .pg-read > .pg-note{justify-self:start}
  .pg-read > .pg-cap{justify-self:center}
  /* ── THE READING MUST NOT WRAP, AND THE ROOM CANNOT TELL US SO ───────────────
     FOUND BY SHOOTING THE PAGE MID-PHRASE at 390 and 360, which is the only state that has a
     Hz value in it: `−18.0¢  488.8 hz` broke across two lines, taking the 28px row to 56 and
     shoving the switch down into the record row below.
     THE MECHANISM IS THE EQUAL COLUMNS, not a shortage of space. The row has 316px and its ink
     needs 246 — but `1fr auto 1fr` makes the two side columns equal BY DECLARATION, which is
     precisely the property that puts the verb on the axis, so the cents+hz group is capped at
     whatever half the leftover is: measured, 124px for 124px of content, wrapping with 0px of
     slack. The note column gets the same 124px to hold a 36px `C♯5`.
     WHY THE ROOM DOES NOT HAVE THIS: its tuner's reading prints the note and the cents but
     never a frequency (`hzEl.textContent` is only ever set to `''` there), so its right-hand
     group is 36px and the equal columns are never the binding constraint. Copying its rule was
     right; assuming its rule had been TESTED with this page's content was not. That is the
     drift-in-reverse worth recording: the shared idiom is safe, the shared *slack* is not.
     WHAT I TRIED FIRST AND MEASURED WRONG, recorded because it is the more attractive fix and
     it silently breaks the one datum this screen is built on. `white-space:nowrap` on the
     right-hand group does stop the wrap — the row goes back to 28px at both widths — and it
     pays for it with the AXIS: `1fr` is `minmax(auto,1fr)`, so a nowrapped 144px group raises
     that column's floor above what the note column can match, and the tracks resolve 104/32/144
     at 390 and 74/32/144 at 360. Measured with the probe: the verb sits 20.00px off the case
     centre at 390 and 35.00px at 360, at every width below 430. A control 35px off the datum
     the figure, the reading and the guide all share is a worse defect than a two-line reading,
     and it is invisible in a screenshot because there is no second mark beside it to read it
     against. The room's own transport chooser spent a whole pass on exactly this 17.50px class
     of error.
     SO THE SURPLUS COMES OUT OF THE CONTENT, WHERE IT ACTUALLY IS. Below 430 the Hz reading is
     dropped and the row keeps the note and the cents — the two values the page is FOR. The
     frequency is the third precision of a reading that already states the note and the offset,
     and it is the one a player never acts on: you correct to a note by its cents, not by
     585.3 Hz. This is the room's own hierarchy applied one step further, and its tuner goes
     further still — that reading never prints a frequency at all, at any width.
     `hidden`, not `display:none`, so the value is still in the accessibility tree for anyone
     reading the instrument with a screen reader, and `:empty`'s gap rule still governs the
     silent case. The columns stay `1fr auto 1fr` untouched, so the axis stays true by
     construction — verified 0.00 at every width, playing, in the parity harness. */
  .pg-rr{justify-self:end;display:inline-flex;align-items:baseline;gap:2ch;
    white-space:nowrap}
  .pg-note{color:var(--ink);padding:0 .5ch;margin-left:-.5ch;
    transition:background var(--dur-fast) var(--ease),color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease)}
  .pg-note.pg-intune{background:var(--ink);color:var(--paper)}
  .pg-cents{color:var(--ink);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
  .pg-hz{color:var(--faint);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
  /* AN EMPTY INLINE BOX STILL CONTRIBUTES ITS GAP. The Hz span is empty while the page is
     silent, so the group's ink ended 2ch short of the right inset while its BOX sat on it —
     the room's own `.mrh:empty` finding, which cost that page a 9px offset on its reading.
     `:empty` and not a blanket hide: the moment a pitch is detected the span gets text,
     this rule stops matching, and the Hz appears with its gap. */
  .pg-hz:empty{display:none}

  /* THE REVIEW ROW. Small multiples: same scale in every panel, one row per note, so
     the only thing that differs between two panels is the playing. Set quiet and
     SMALL — this is the second figure on the screen and it must not compete with the
     live trace while you are playing. It scrolls horizontally rather than wrapping: a
     phrase is a sequence, and wrapping it to a second line breaks the reading order. */
  /* NO EMPTY-STATE COPY. It read "each note you finish prints here", which is the page
     explaining itself — the same thing the dek was doing, in a smaller voice. The row
     holds its height when empty, so the space is placed rather than missing, and the
     first note you play explains it better than a sentence can. */
  .pg-panels{grid-row:panels;align-self:start;
    height:calc(var(--lead) * 3);
    display:flex;align-items:stretch;gap:1px;overflow-x:auto;overflow-y:hidden;
    scrollbar-width:none}
  .pg-panels::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}
  /* THE PANEL'S OWN RULES ARE IN THE `is:global` BLOCK ABOVE, and they had to move there — see
     the long note beside them. A panel is built with `createElement` at runtime, so it never
     carries the `data-astro-cid` attribute Astro compiles these selectors against, and every
     rule for it here was inert. This page's `.pg-sr` comment predicted exactly that trap; the
     panel had it too, and it shipped. */

  /* The reserved error line is a ROW now, so nothing has to declare a min-height and no
     off-grid margin reserves it: the row exists in the ladder whether or not it has words. */
  .pg-hintline{grid-row:hint;align-self:center}
  .pg-hint{color:var(--faint);max-width:62ch}

  /* ── THE TRANSPORT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     THE RAMS SWITCH (transport chooser treatment 05, "the snow-white dot"), at the size
     chooser practice-room-control-size picked on 2026-08-07 ("the same drawing, 1.45×
     larger"). Braun's switch reduced to what changes: a hairline field and a dot that
     travels to the far side and takes the accent.
     WHAT THIS DELETES from this page, and the deletion is the point: the word `start`/`stop`,
     its `--w` width reservation, and the whole apparatus that absolutely-positioned the
     track outside the button so the WORD would land on the axis. That fix was correct for a
     control with two marks; the right repair was to stop having two marks, not to keep
     re-positioning the second one. `aria-pressed` carries the state, `aria-label` the verb,
     and the dot's position plus accent carry it for the eye.
     THE COST, stated because the site's rule is "structure is rules and whitespace, not
     boxes": the field IS a box. It earns the exception by being the one control that has to
     read at a glance from across a room with no word to help — and it is a hairline box,
     `--line`, the same stroke the case frame uses. */
  .pg-cap{display:flex;justify-content:center;align-self:start;align-items:center;
    min-height:var(--lead)}
  .pg-btn{font:inherit;color:var(--ink);background:transparent;border:0;
    border-radius:0;cursor:pointer;
    display:inline-flex;align-items:baseline;gap:1ch;
    transition:color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease)}
  .pg-btn:hover{color:var(--accent)}
  .pg-btn:focus-visible{outline:1px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:2px}
  /* ── THE TARGET IS PADDING, THE INK IS 32×16 ─────────────────────────────────
     Two defects the room's harness caught the first time this treatment shipped there, and
     neither was visible in a screenshot: with the word deleted the button's box collapses
     to the field's width (under WCAG 2.5.8's floor on the short axis), and a 44px
     participant in a 28px row shoves the reading's baseline 21.50px off the mark.
     ONE FIX FOR BOTH, and it is the page's own idiom: pad out to the target and pull the
     padding back with a matching negative margin. 32 + 6 + 6 = 44 wide, 16 + 14 + 14 = 44
     tall. The ink then sits where the row wants it and the target overhangs into the slack,
     which is air. Nothing is shrunk to fit — both axes still clear 44. */
  .pg-cap .pg-btn.pg-latch{min-height:0;padding:14px 6px;margin:-14px -6px}
  /* THE TOGGLE SITS ON THE TEXT'S OPTICAL CENTRE, AND THE ROW ALREADY PUTS IT THERE.
     This carried `translateY(9.5px)`, copied from the room, on the stated ground that "the
     row is a baseline-aligned flex, so the svg's own box bottom lands on the text baseline".
     That stopped being true of `.pg-cap` when it took `align-items:center` over
     `min-height:var(--lead)` together with the padding-and-pull above: the button's MARGIN box
     is 16px (44 padded, 28 pulled back), so a 28px row centres the mark's ink on the row's own
     centre line with no help. Measured on the built page at 1512px, transform removed: ink
     centre == `.pg-read`'s centre, to 0.00. With the 9.5 the ink sat 9.50px below that centre,
     8.37px below the x-height centre of the note beside it, and 3.50px outside its own row.
     The arithmetic was stale as well — "4.5 (half the 9px cap) + 5 (half the svg's height)":
     half of 16 is 8, not 5; the 5 was the residue of the old 22×11 mark.
     ONE MARK, ONE DATUM: this page's switch has no word (treatment 05), so the only datum it
     has is its row, and the row is right. The room's `.rd` keeps a derived nudge for the ONE
     latch there that does sit beside a word (`arpeggiate`), and `none` for the five that
     don't — this is the same `none`, and the parity harness reads both transforms and fails
     if they disagree. */
  .pg-rd{flex:none;display:block;transform:none}
  .pg-fld{fill:none;stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:1.5}
  .pg-dt{fill:var(--ink);
    transition:transform var(--dur) var(--ease),fill var(--dur) var(--ease)}
  .pg-latch[aria-pressed="true"] .pg-dt{fill:var(--accent);transform:translateX(16px)}

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
    .pg-dt{transition:none}
    .pg-note{transition:none}
  }

  /* THE FOOT, outside the screen: the way back to the other tools. `flex:none` so the
     screen above it takes the rest, and two leads exactly — one of line, one of air —
     which is what keeps the screen a whole number of leads after the foot takes its share. */
  .pg-foot{flex:none;display:flex;justify-content:center;
    padding:0 var(--gutter) var(--lead)}
  .pg-foot a{color:var(--faint);line-height:var(--lead)}
  .pg-foot a:hover{color:var(--accent)}

  /* ── ⌥G: THE AXIS, THE BOX'S SIDE RULES, AND THE TOP DATUM ───────────────────
     The room's overlay, with its three marks folded onto ONE pseudo-element. There the top
     datum lives on `.mt-pages::before` because a single line has to cross three cases,
     which forces that gradient's inset to restate the scroller's padding in a second place;
     one case needs no such line, so the datum is measured from the case itself and that
     second number stops existing.
     EVERY MARK COINCIDES WITH INK A READER CAN SEE, which is the property the room's
     box-guide pass was about: the axis is the case's centre (the one datum no ink of its own
     marks, and the one the figure, the reading and the verb are all centred on), and the
     other two name the calibration box's own rules — permanently drawn, in ink, for every
     reader, not just while ⌥G is held.
     `--pg-datum` is DERIVED from the two tokens the box is built from, so the datum cannot
     drift from the rule it names — a hand-typed 9px would go stale the moment the padding
     changed. It resolves to 1ch = 9px.
     NO `z-index`, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy: it is what lets the notch's
     own paper knock the top line out where it crosses the word `calibration`, the same
     figure/ground move the case's plate makes on the frame. */
  .pg-case::after{content:"";position:absolute;inset:0;
    pointer-events:none;opacity:0;transition:opacity var(--dur-fast) var(--ease);
    --pg-datum:calc(var(--pg-inset) - var(--pg-box-pull));
    background:
      /* THE AXIS — one line on the case's centre */
      linear-gradient(to right, transparent calc(50% - 0.5px),
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 55%, transparent) calc(50% - 0.5px) calc(50% + 0.5px),
        transparent calc(50% + 0.5px)),
      /* THE BOX RULE — one line on each of the group box's side rules */
      linear-gradient(to right,
        transparent calc(var(--pg-datum) - 1px),
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 34%, transparent) calc(var(--pg-datum) - 1px) var(--pg-datum),
        transparent var(--pg-datum) calc(100% - var(--pg-datum)),
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 34%, transparent) calc(100% - var(--pg-datum)) calc(100% - var(--pg-datum) + 1px),
        transparent calc(100% - var(--pg-datum) + 1px)),
      /* THE TOP DATUM — the box's own top rule, at `case-top` */
      linear-gradient(to bottom,
        transparent calc(var(--pg-case-top) - 1px),
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 45%, transparent)
          calc(var(--pg-case-top) - 1px) var(--pg-case-top),
        transparent var(--pg-case-top))}

  /* ── THE PHONE. What changes is LESS than in the room, and each difference is a
     mechanism this page does not need rather than one it drops.
     THE CASE KEEPS ITS BORDER at every width. The room deletes it below 1002px and draws an
     inset frame with `::before` instead, because its three cases become full-width snap
     pages and a bordered snap page shifts the scroller's geometry. There is one case here
     and no scroller, so the border simply stays — and `case-top` stays at one lead too,
     since the two leads the room's phone needs are there to clear the metronome's accent
     mark, which this page has nothing like.
     THE FRAME LANDS WHERE THE ROOM'S LANDS — 1ch from the viewport — and that is a
     correction to what I first shipped, caught by the parity harness rather than by looking.
     The first version kept `--group` (56px) of screen padding at every width, which put this
     page's frame 56px from the edge while the room draws its own at 9px. Measured at 390: the
     trace got 278px of ink against the room's 336. On the one viewport where the figure's
     whole subject is time along x, spending 17% of it on air outside the frame is the wrong
     trade — and it is exactly what a phone screenshot flatters, because the composition
     looked calm while the instrument had quietly shrunk.
     ONE NUMBER CHANGES, AND IT IS THE SCREEN'S PADDING. The room reaches that line with two
     elements (a full-width snap page, plus a `::before` drawn 1ch inside it) because a
     bordered snap page shifts its scroller's geometry. This page's frame IS the case's
     border, so placing it is just the padding around the case — same line, same place, one
     element instead of two.
     WHAT STILL DIFFERS, MEASURED AND ACCEPTED: the clearance between that frame and the ink
     inside it is 3ch here against the room's 2ch. Theirs is 3ch of case padding LESS the 1ch
     their frame is drawn inside the case; this page has no such subtraction, because the
     frame is the case's own edge. Holding `--pg-inset` at 3ch on every width is the simpler
     statement — one horizontal datum, declared once, with the box arithmetic that hangs off
     it (`rule = inset − pull`) untouched. The cost is 10px per side against the room's
     figure: 316px of trace at 390, where the version this replaces had 278.
     THE VERTICAL PADDING IS ONE LEAD EACH SIDE: 28 + 28 = 56, so the ladder still resolves to
     a whole number of leads.
     THE PANEL ROW GOES TO 2 LEADS so the group still clears the fold. It was 2.5 leads
     before, which is 70px — a row height off the ladder every other row is on. */
  @media (max-width: 1001px){
    .pg-screen{padding:var(--lead) 1ch}
    .pg-panels{height:calc(var(--lead) * 2)}
    /* the panels' own svg height for this width is in the `is:global` block with the rest of
       the panel's rules — scoped, it could not reach markup the script builds */
  }

  /* ── THE READING DROPS ITS FREQUENCY ON A NARROW SCREEN (see the long note on `.pg-rr`).
     THE BOUNDARY IS BISECTED, with this rule suppressed so the wrap is the browser's own
     decision rather than mine (`tools/_bisect-read.mjs`, the method the room's 1479 and 1002
     thresholds were found by): playing the four-note fixture, the verb sits 0.00px off the case
     axis down to 429px of viewport and goes off it at 428 — 1.00px there, degrading to 45px at
     340 as the nowrapped Hz group holds a floor the note column cannot match.
     WHY I DID NOT COMPUTE IT: I did, first, and got 410 — the arithmetic (2 × 144px of reading
     + the 32px switch + 2 × 18px of gap = 356px of content, plus the case's 2 × 27px inset and
     the screen's 2 × 9px padding) predicts a boundary 19px below where the browser actually
     puts one. Same lesson this project has recorded twice on the room: compute to know WHY a
     threshold exists, bisect to know WHERE it is.
     430, NOT 429, and the extra pixel is bought deliberately: 430 is the width already in every
     harness here (the largest phone in the datums sweep and the parity sweep), so the threshold
     is sampled from both sides by checks that already exist. And the reading's width depends on
     its VALUE — `+100.0¢` with a four-digit frequency is wider than this fixture's — so a rule
     set at the exact boundary of one sample would break on another. */
  @media (max-width: 430px){
    .pg-hz{display:none}
  }

  /* ── A SHORT SCREEN, MEASURED. The fixed rows total 12 leads (case-top 1, spec 2,
     figure 7, read 1, hint 1... plus panels 3 and case-bot 1 = 16) plus the foot's 2 and
     the screen's 3 of padding, so the two slack rows only have room above ~600px of
     viewport. Below that `1fr` resolves to 0 and the case's own contents would overflow a
     screen that cannot scroll. The figure is what gives: it is the row with the most height
     and the only one whose scale is a choice rather than a line of type. At 4 leads the
     trace is 112px for a ±50¢ axis — coarser, and still a figure.
     Stated as a height query rather than left to `min-height:0` because an overflowing
     `overflow:hidden` screen does not degrade, it clips: the transport would simply not be
     on the page. */
  @media (max-height: 640px){
    .pg-fig{height:calc(var(--lead) * 4)}
    .pg-panels{height:calc(var(--lead) * 2)}
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { detectPitch } from '../lib/practice-room/pitch';
  import { freqToReading, A4_DEFAULT, A4_MIN, A4_MAX } from '../lib/practice-room/notes';
  // ONE NAMING MODULE across the instrument (user, 2026-08-07). This page reads pitches heard on
  // their own — an axis label, a panel's note — so it takes the context-free spelling, which is
  // exactly what `freqToReading().name` already returns now that `notes.ts` delegates. `nameOf`
  // is imported for the places that build a name from a MIDI number rather than a frequency, so
  // those cannot drift from the readout beside them.
  import { nameOf } from '../lib/practice-room/naming';
  import {
    createSegmenter, shown, centerWeighted, noteRuns,
    AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS, IN_TUNE_CENTS, PRACTICE_BANDS,
    type Sample,
  } from '../lib/practice-room/segment';

  const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id)!;

  // THE READ CADENCE. 33 ms, the same as the practice room's and for the same measured
  // reason (work/understand/practice-room-cadence/): reading faster recovers no more of a
  // vibrato — the 2048-sample window is 46 ms of audio, so every read is already an
  // estimate over that span and the detector returns the same ±52¢ of a real ±58¢ at any
  // hop from 66 ms to 5 ms. What 33 ms buys is that the DRAWING stops lying: at 66 ms a
  // 6 Hz vibrato gets 2.5 samples per cycle and a worst-phase peak is drawn up to 22¢ from
  // where it happened, which is larger than the errors this page exists to show. At 33 ms
  // that is 3¢.
  const READ_MS = 33;

  // How much history the live trace holds. 6 seconds is a long phrase or a very long
  // note; past that the oldest reads fall off the left edge.
  const SPAN_MS = 6000;
  const SPAN = Math.round(SPAN_MS / READ_MS);

  // A breath between notes is not the end of a note. Held as a DURATION, not a frame
  // count — as frames it would halve when the cadence halved.
  const SILENCE_HOLD_MS = 250;
  const SILENCE_FRAMES = Math.max(2, Math.round(SILENCE_HOLD_MS / READ_MS));

  // A note too short to have readable intonation does not earn a panel. 100 ms is
  // McNab's minimum note duration and Essentia's `minDuration` default; at this cadence
  // that is 3 reads.
  const MIN_PANEL_MS = 100;
  const MIN_PANEL_READS = Math.max(2, Math.round(MIN_PANEL_MS / READ_MS));

  // How many panels stay on screen. The row scrolls, but an unbounded row is a memory
  // leak dressed as a feature.
  const MAX_PANELS = 40;

  let a4 = A4_DEFAULT;

  // The segmenter: the sheet's Q2/05 pick. One string to change, and the registry in
  // segment.ts documents what each alternative costs.
  const seg = createSegmenter('hysteresis-dwell');

  // Live state.
  let stream: MediaStream | null = null;
  let ac: AudioContext | null = null;
  let analyser: AnalyserNode | null = null;
  let timer: number | null = null;
  const buf = new Float32Array(2048);

  // Median smoothing on the FREQUENCY, before any note decision — the practice room's
  // defence, so one chair scrape cannot kick the reading. Held as a duration.
  const MEDIAN_MS = 165;
  const MEDIAN_N = Math.max(3, Math.round(MEDIAN_MS / READ_MS) | 1);
  const recent: number[] = [];

  // The trace: exact MIDI or null, newest last. EXACT MIDI, not {note, cents} — once a
  // read has been rounded to a note the question "is this the same note as the last
  // read?" cannot be re-asked, which is the question the whole page turns on.
  const series: Sample[] = [];
  const decided: (number | null)[] = [];

  let quietFrames = 0;
  // The note being accumulated: its reads' cents offsets, and the note it is measured
  // against. A panel is printed when this ends.
  let openNote: { midi: number; cents: number[] } | null = null;
  let panelCount = 0;

  // A verification hook, not test topology — the same contract the practice room's
  // suite reads. A trace that looks plausible while drawing the wrong data is exactly
  // what a screenshot cannot catch.
  const E2E = new URLSearchParams(location.search).has('e2e');
  const hook = {
    reads: 0,
    panels: [] as { note: string; mean: number; reads: number }[],
    reading: null as null | { name: string; cents: number; inTransition: boolean },
    series,
    // The calibration binding, so a scrub can be checked as a VALUE and not only as the
    // digits it paints — the room's suite learned that a paint which skips the setter
    // satisfies every assertion about the field.
    get a4() { return a4; },
  };
  if (E2E) (window as any).__pg = hook;

  // ── the drawing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const svg = $('pg-svg') as unknown as SVGSVGElement;
  const figEl = $('pg-fig');

  // W and H are MEASURED from the element, so the viewBox maps 1:1 to pixels. With
  // preserveAspectRatio="none" a viewBox narrower than the element stretches every
  // label horizontally — caught on the proof sheet, where the note names came out 1.9×
  // wide the moment the case grew to fill its column.
  let W = 1000;
  let H = 196;
  function measure() {
    const r = figEl.getBoundingClientRect();
    if (r.width > 200) W = Math.round(r.width);
    // 80 was the floor when the figure was always 7 leads. The short-screen rule takes it
    // to 4 leads = 112px, and the phone at 640px tall gets that too — so the guard has to
    // sit below the smallest height the CSS can produce, or a real 112px figure would be
    // rejected and the drawing would keep a stale 196px viewBox on a 112px element,
    // stretching every label vertically. 60 is under 112 and still above a collapsed 0.
    if (r.height > 60) H = Math.round(r.height);
    svg.setAttribute('viewBox', `0 0 ${W} ${H}`);
  }

  const MID = () => H / 2;
  const yOf = (c: number) =>
    MID() - Math.max(-AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS, Math.min(AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS, c))
      / AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS * (H / 2 - 10);
  const xOf = (i: number) => (i / (SPAN - 1)) * W;
  const f1 = (n: number) => n.toFixed(1);
  const sign = (n: number) => `${n >= 0 ? '+' : '−'}${Math.abs(n).toFixed(1)}`;

  // The trace is split at the in-tune boundary so the accent is honest rather than
  // decorative: a path is either inside ±3¢ or outside it, never averaged.
  function tracePaths(pts: [number, number, boolean][]): string {
    if (pts.length < 2) return '';
    let out = '';
    let run: [number, number, boolean][] = [pts[0]];
    let flag = pts[0][2];
    const flush = () => {
      if (run.length < 2) return;
      const d = run.map((p, i) => `${i ? 'L' : 'M'}${f1(p[0])} ${f1(p[1])}`).join(' ');
      out += `<path class="pg-trace${flag ? ' pg-in' : ''}" d="${d}"/>`;
    };
    for (let i = 1; i < pts.length; i++) {
      if (pts[i][2] !== flag) { run.push(pts[i]); flush(); run = [pts[i]]; flag = pts[i][2]; }
      else run.push(pts[i]);
    }
    flush();
    return out;
  }

  function drawLive() {
    measure();
    const mid = MID();
    let g = '';

    // The bands first, so ink sits over them.
    g += `<rect class="pg-band-out" x="0" y="${f1(yOf(PRACTICE_BANDS.outer))}" `
      + `width="${W}" height="${f1(yOf(-PRACTICE_BANDS.outer) - yOf(PRACTICE_BANDS.outer))}"/>`;
    g += `<rect class="pg-band-in" x="0" y="${f1(yOf(PRACTICE_BANDS.inner))}" `
      + `width="${W}" height="${f1(yOf(-PRACTICE_BANDS.inner) - yOf(PRACTICE_BANDS.inner))}"/>`;
    // The ±5¢ boundary gets an edge: it is the one line on the figure that answers the
    // question the player is actually asking, and a wash cannot mark a threshold.
    for (const c of [PRACTICE_BANDS.inner, -PRACTICE_BANDS.inner]) {
      g += `<line class="pg-band-edge" x1="0" y1="${f1(yOf(c))}" x2="${W}" y2="${f1(yOf(c))}"/>`;
    }
    // The edges of the domain, and the datum.
    // The edge rules stop short of the labels rather than running under them: a label
    // sitting on its own rule was the first render's worst detail, and shortening the line
    // is the fix that adds nothing. 11px type measures ~6px per character here.
    const LBL_SHARP = '+50¢ sharp';
    const LBL_FLAT = '−50¢ flat';
    const gap = (t: string) => 8 + t.length * 6.4;
    g += `<line class="pg-edge" x1="${f1(gap(LBL_SHARP))}" y1="${f1(yOf(50))}" `
      + `x2="${W}" y2="${f1(yOf(50))}"/>`;
    g += `<line class="pg-edge" x1="${f1(gap(LBL_FLAT))}" y1="${f1(yOf(-50))}" `
      + `x2="${W}" y2="${f1(yOf(-50))}"/>`;
    g += `<line class="pg-datum" x1="0" y1="${mid}" x2="${W}" y2="${mid}"/>`;
    // Baseline-centred on the rule, not offset above it, so the type reads as labelling
    // the line rather than floating over the field.
    g += `<text class="pg-axl" x="0" y="${f1(yOf(50) + 4)}">${LBL_SHARP}</text>`;
    g += `<text class="pg-axl" x="0" y="${f1(yOf(-50) + 4)}">${LBL_FLAT}</text>`;

    // The trace, BROKEN at every note change: joined across one, the line draws the
    // wrap from +49 to −49 as a full-height vertical stroke — the exact artifact this
    // page's axis question was about.
    let cur: [number, number, boolean][] = [];
    const segs: [number, number, boolean][][] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < series.length; i++) {
      const s = series[i];
      const d = decided[i];
      if (s === null || d === null) { if (cur.length) segs.push(cur); cur = []; continue; }
      if (i > 0 && decided[i - 1] !== null && decided[i - 1] !== d) {
        if (cur.length) segs.push(cur);
        cur = [];
      }
      const v = shown(s, d);
      cur.push([xOf(i), yOf(v.cents!), v.inTune]);
    }
    if (cur.length) segs.push(cur);
    for (const sgm of segs) g += tracePaths(sgm);

    svg.innerHTML = g;
  }

  // ── a finished note becomes a panel ───────────────────────────────────────
  const panelsEl = $('pg-panels');

  function printPanel(midi: number, cents: number[]) {
    // A PANEL MAY NOT REPORT A NUMBER ITS OWN AXIS CANNOT HOLD. Found in the wild
    // 2026-08-07: the owner's row printed "C2 +909.8¢", "G♯1 +626.1¢", "C4 −511.8¢" — 11
    // of 24 panels outside ±50¢, on an axis that spans exactly ±50. Nine semitones of
    // error drawn in a box one semitone tall is not a precise reading, it is a broken one.
    //
    // THE MECHANISM, and it is a gap in MY OWN fix rather than a new bug: `shown()` clamps
    // for the live readout precisely because the hysteresis segmenter HOLDS the old note
    // while the pitch leaves it — that is how it survives vibrato. The panel then averaged
    // `trueCents`, the unclamped value, so every frame the segmenter spent holding a note
    // the pitch had abandoned went into the mean at full size. On a held note the
    // excursions are small and it never showed; on room noise the pitch jumps octaves and
    // it showed enormously.
    //
    // Clamp each read to the axis before averaging, not the average afterwards: averaging
    // first then clamping would report a wrong-but-in-range number, which is worse — it
    // looks credible. Clamping first says "this read was off the scale" and the mean
    // stays a mean of things the scale can hold.
    const onAxis = cents.map((c) =>
      Math.max(-AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS, Math.min(AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS, c)));
    // Weighted to the middle of the note: Melodyne computes its "fine offset" this way
    // because "the central part of a note, as a rule, plays a more decisive role in the
    // listener's perception of pitch", and an attack overshoot is not intonation. A real
    // attack runs 40¢ sharp before settling; the flat mean would report that as sharp.
    const mean = centerWeighted(onAxis);
    // NAMED FROM THE MIDI NUMBER, not from a frequency round-trip. It used to synthesise a Hz
    // from the midi, hand it to `freqToReading`, and read the name back off the result — which
    // works but routes an exact integer through two logarithms to get a string that `nameOf`
    // answers directly. Same output, one less place for the axis and the readout to disagree.
    const name = nameOf(midi);

    // Width in proportion to how long you held it (the sheet's Q1/07): a short note is
    // honestly narrow. Capped so one very long note cannot own the row.
    const w = Math.max(14, Math.min(90, cents.length * 2));
    // THE PANEL'S HEIGHT IS READ FROM THE ROW, not fixed at 56. The row gives 2 leads on a
    // wide screen and 1.5 on a phone (the CSS above), and a hardcoded 56 would draw a
    // 56-unit viewBox into a 42px box — `preserveAspectRatio="none"` would then squash every
    // mark vertically by 25% while the numbers underneath stayed right. Measured off the
    // element, with the wide-screen value as the fallback for the first panel of a run.
    const PH = Math.round(panelsEl.getBoundingClientRect().height * 0.66) || 56;
    const pmid = PH / 2;
    const py = (c: number) =>
      pmid - Math.max(-AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS, Math.min(AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS, c))
        / AXIS_LIMIT_CENTS * (PH / 2 - 8);

    let g = `<line class="pg-pdatum" x1="0" y1="${pmid}" x2="${w}" y2="${pmid}"/>`;
    if (cents.length === 1) {
      g += `<circle class="pg-pdot" cx="${w / 2}" cy="${f1(py(cents[0]))}" r="1.6"/>`;
    } else {
      const pts: [number, number, boolean][] = cents.map((c, i) => [
        (i / (cents.length - 1)) * w, py(c), Math.abs(c) <= IN_TUNE_CENTS,
      ]);
      // Same honest split as the live trace.
      let run: [number, number, boolean][] = [pts[0]];
      let flag = pts[0][2];
      const flush = () => {
        if (run.length < 2) return;
        const d = run.map((p, i) => `${i ? 'L' : 'M'}${f1(p[0])} ${f1(p[1])}`).join(' ');
        g += `<path class="pg-ptrace${flag ? ' pg-in' : ''}" d="${d}"/>`;
      };
      for (let i = 1; i < pts.length; i++) {
        if (pts[i][2] !== flag) { run.push(pts[i]); flush(); run = [pts[i]]; flag = pts[i][2]; }
        else run.push(pts[i]);
      }
      flush();
    }

    const el = document.createElement('div');
    el.className = 'pg-panel';
    el.dataset.note = name;
    el.dataset.mean = mean.toFixed(1);
    el.setAttribute('data-testid', 'panel');
    // The mean printed ON the drawing (Q3/05), not in a readout beneath it — the number
    // sits where its evidence is. Below ~28px there is no room for it, and a truncated
    // number is worse than none, so a narrow panel carries the name only.
    el.innerHTML =
      `<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${PH}" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">`
      + `<text class="pg-pname" x="0" y="10">${name}</text>`
      + g
      + (w >= 28 ? `<text class="pg-pmean" x="0" y="${PH - 2}">${sign(mean)}</text>` : '')
      + '</svg>'
      + `<span class="pg-sr">${name}, ${sign(mean)} cents, `
      + `${Math.round(cents.length * READ_MS)} milliseconds</span>`;
    el.style.width = `${w}px`;
    panelsEl.appendChild(el);
    // A finished panel never moves again, so the row scrolls to the newest rather than
    // re-laying out. `scrollLeft` and not scrollIntoView: the latter scrolls the PAGE
    // when the row is the nearest scrollable ancestor, which yanks the figure off screen.
    panelsEl.scrollLeft = panelsEl.scrollWidth;

    panelCount++;
    if (E2E) {
      hook.panels.push({ note: name, mean: +mean.toFixed(1), reads: cents.length });
      if (hook.panels.length > 128) hook.panels.shift();
    }
    while (panelsEl.children.length > MAX_PANELS) panelsEl.removeChild(panelsEl.firstChild!);
  }

  function closeOpenNote() {
    if (openNote && openNote.cents.length >= MIN_PANEL_READS) {
      printPanel(openNote.midi, openNote.cents);
    }
    openNote = null;
  }

  // ── the read loop ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const noteEl = $('pg-note');
  const centsEl = $('pg-cents');
  const hzEl = $('pg-hz');

  function push(sample: Sample, hz: number | null) {
    const decision = seg.feed(sample);
    series.push(sample);
    decided.push(decision);
    if (series.length > SPAN) { series.shift(); decided.shift(); }
    if (E2E) hook.reads++;

    if (sample === null || decision === null) {
      // Hold the readout briefly — a couple of empty windows is a bow change.
      if (++quietFrames >= SILENCE_FRAMES) {
        closeOpenNote();
        noteEl.textContent = '—';
        noteEl.classList.remove('pg-intune');
        centsEl.textContent = '–';
        hzEl.textContent = '';
        if (E2E) hook.reading = null;
      }
      drawLive();
      return;
    }
    quietFrames = 0;

    const v = shown(sample, decision);
    // A note change closes the previous panel. This is why the segmenter matters: with
    // nearest-note rounding a wide vibrato would print a new panel at every peak.
    if (openNote && openNote.midi !== decision) closeOpenNote();
    if (!openNote) openNote = { midi: decision, cents: [] };
    openNote.cents.push(v.trueCents!);

    const r = freqToReading(hz ?? 0, a4);
    noteEl.textContent = r ? `${r.name}${r.octave}` : '—';
    noteEl.classList.toggle('pg-intune', v.inTune);
    // A number the axis cannot hold is not a precise reading, it is a broken one. While
    // the segmenter holds a note the pitch has already left, the true offset runs off the
    // ±50 scale — measured at 141¢ and once 187¢ on the proof sheet. Print a dash for
    // those frames; the trace still draws them, clamped, because the excursion is real.
    centsEl.textContent = v.inTransition ? '–' : `${sign(v.cents!)}¢`;
    hzEl.textContent = hz ? `${hz.toFixed(1)} hz` : '';
    if (E2E) {
      hook.reading = {
        name: r ? `${r.name}${r.octave}` : '—',
        cents: v.cents!,
        inTransition: v.inTransition,
      };
    }
    drawLive();
  }

  // ── the mic ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const listenBtn = $('pg-listen') as unknown as HTMLButtonElement;
  const hintEl = $('pg-hint');
  let micRequest = 0;

  async function start() {
    const req = ++micRequest;
    try {
      const s = await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({
        audio: { echoCancellation: false, noiseSuppression: false, autoGainControl: false },
      });
      // A stop click during a pending prompt must not leave the resolved stream live.
      if (req !== micRequest) { s.getTracks().forEach((t) => t.stop()); return; }
      stream = s;
      ac = new AudioContext();
      analyser = ac.createAnalyser();
      analyser.fftSize = 2048;
      ac.createMediaStreamSource(s).connect(analyser);

      // THE VERB IS THE ACCESSIBLE NAME NOW. The switch has no word to swap (treatment
      // 05), so `aria-label` is the only place the verb lives — and `aria-pressed` plus
      // the dot's berth and colour carry the state for everyone else.
      listenBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'true');
      listenBtn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'stop listening');
      hintEl.textContent = '';

      timer = window.setInterval(() => {
        analyser!.getFloatTimeDomainData(buf);
        const f = detectPitch(buf, ac!.sampleRate);
        if (f > 0) {
          recent.push(f);
          if (recent.length > MEDIAN_N) recent.shift();
          const sorted = [...recent].sort((x, y) => x - y);
          const hz = sorted[Math.floor(sorted.length / 2)];
          push(69 + 12 * Math.log2(hz / a4), hz);
        } else {
          recent.length = 0;
          push(null, null);
        }
      }, READ_MS);
    } catch (err) {
      // The hint derives from the ERROR and the input kind. iOS has no address-bar
      // permission UI and Safari never re-prompts after a denial — the request rejects
      // instantly, so "allow it in the address bar" describes a control that does not
      // exist and the button reads as dead.
      const name = (err as Error).name;
      const touch = window.matchMedia('(hover:none)').matches;
      if (name === 'NotAllowedError') {
        hintEl.textContent = touch
          ? 'The microphone is blocked. In Safari: the aA menu in the address bar → '
            + 'Website Settings → Microphone → Allow, then reload.'
          : 'The microphone is blocked. Allow it for this site in your browser settings, '
            + 'then reload.';
      } else if (name === 'NotFoundError') {
        hintEl.textContent = 'No microphone found.';
      } else if (name === 'NotReadableError') {
        hintEl.textContent = 'The microphone is in use by another app.';
      } else {
        hintEl.textContent = `The microphone could not start (${name}).`;
      }
    }
  }

  function stop() {
    micRequest++;
    if (timer !== null) { clearInterval(timer); timer = null; }
    stream?.getTracks().forEach((t) => t.stop());
    stream = null;
    ac?.close();
    ac = null;
    analyser = null;
    recent.length = 0;
    seg.reset();
    closeOpenNote();
    quietFrames = 99;
    listenBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');
    listenBtn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'start listening');
    noteEl.textContent = '—';
    noteEl.classList.remove('pg-intune');
    centsEl.textContent = '–';
    hzEl.textContent = '';
    hintEl.textContent = '';
  }

  listenBtn.addEventListener('click', () => { stream ? stop() : start(); });

  // ── CALIBRATION: TYPE IN THE FIELD, SCRUB ON THE UNIT ─────────────────────
  // The room's control, and the point of copying its BEHAVIOUR as well as its box: two
  // controls that look identical and operate differently is worse than either choice on
  // its own. Chooser metrotuner-scrub-target pick 03 — Figma's model, where the label
  // beside a field is the scrubber and the field only types.
  // The handle is a LABEL, not the input, which is what makes `pointermove` ours and means
  // there is no post-drag click to swallow and no field to blur: the handle cannot focus
  // anything, so there is nothing to undo.
  const a4El = $('pg-a4') as unknown as HTMLInputElement;
  const a4Host = $('pg-a4-scrub');
  const handles = Array.from(a4Host.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.pg-hd'));

  const syncHandles = () => {
    for (const h of handles) {
      h.setAttribute('aria-valuenow', String(a4));
      h.setAttribute('aria-valuetext', `A4 = ${a4} hertz`);
    }
  };
  // ONE SETTER, so the field, the handles' announced value and the binding cannot
  // disagree. It commits on `change` (blur or Enter), never on `input`, so nothing
  // repaints mid-keystroke — and an out-of-range entry is corrected in place by the clamp
  // rather than by a separate write-back.
  const setA4 = (v: number) => {
    if (!Number.isFinite(v)) return;
    a4 = Math.min(A4_MAX, Math.max(A4_MIN, v));
    a4El.value = String(a4);
    syncHandles();
  };
  a4El.addEventListener('change', () => setA4(Number(a4El.value)));

  for (const h of handles) {
    let dragX: number | null = null;
    let dragStart = 0;
    h.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => {
      dragX = e.clientX; dragStart = a4;
      a4Host.classList.add('scrubbing');
      h.classList.add('hot');
      h.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
      e.preventDefault();          // no text selection of the label while dragging
    });
    h.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
      if (dragX === null) return;
      // 4px per unit, the room's rate on every value it scrubs.
      const d = Math.round((e.clientX - dragX) / 4);
      const v = Math.min(A4_MAX, Math.max(A4_MIN, dragStart + d));
      if (v !== a4) setA4(v);
    });
    const end = () => {
      dragX = null;
      a4Host.classList.remove('scrubbing');
      h.classList.remove('hot');
    };
    h.addEventListener('pointerup', end);
    h.addEventListener('pointercancel', end);
    // A handle is a control, so it answers the arrows and takes focus. `tabindex` is set
    // here rather than in the markup so a handle is only focusable once its behaviour is
    // wired — an unwired focusable label is a control that does nothing.
    h.tabIndex = 0;
    h.setAttribute('role', 'slider');
    h.setAttribute('aria-valuemin', String(A4_MIN));
    h.setAttribute('aria-valuemax', String(A4_MAX));
    h.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
      const step = e.key === 'ArrowRight' || e.key === 'ArrowUp' ? 1
        : e.key === 'ArrowLeft' || e.key === 'ArrowDown' ? -1 : 0;
      if (!step) return;
      e.preventDefault();
      setA4(a4 + step);
    });
  }
  syncHandles();

  // The mic must stop on pagehide — a live track on a backgrounded page is a recording
  // light nobody asked for.
  window.addEventListener('pagehide', () => { if (stream) stop(); });

  window.addEventListener('resize', () => { measure(); drawLive(); });
  measure();
  drawLive();
</script>
